ive features and a growing list of member rewards.\n Explore \n An account already exists for this email address, please log in.\n (Image credit: Crucial | WD_Black) \n Jump To: \nQuick links\n1 TB\n2 TB\n4 TB or more\nExternal SSDs\nUK SSD deals\nUK external SSD deals\nCopy link\nFacebook\nX\nReddit\nPinterest\nFlipboard\nEmail\n Share this article \n 0 \n Join the conversation \nFollow us\n Add us as a preferred source on Google \nNewsletter\n Subscribe to our newsletter \n Looking for a decent saving on an SSD? I should be able to help, then, as I regularly sweep the web for the best deals on solid state drives. I train my beady little eyes on Amazon, Newegg, Best Buy, Overclockers, and more. You can check out my finds below.\n Join us on WhatsApp for daily deals , direct to your phone. \n It is a weird time for storage. It's in high demand, but not from us gamers. It's the enterprise lot buying up every drive they can find to fulfil the massive requirements of artificial intelligence. It sucks for us lot, to be honest, but that means there's only more need for someone to come along and highlight actual savings on decent SSDs. That's what I'm here for.\n I'm doing my best to find any reasonable discount on a solid state drive today. Though, you should know, prices are much higher now than they were just 12 months ago. I can maybe find you the best price, but that isn't going to be the best price historically. It sucks, I know.\n SSD deals — quick links \n 💾 1 TB SATA: Team Group QX |  $139 @ Newegg \n 💾 1 TB PCIe 4.0 NVMe: Crucial P510 | $169 @ Newegg \n 💾 1 TB PCIe 4.0 NVMe: Biwin NV7400 | $175 @ Amazon \n Amazon - Some of the lowest prices you'll find on SSDs \n Walmart - Deals on internal and external SSDs \n B&H Photo - Hundreds off external drives \n Best Buy - Great prices on Samsung and WD SSDs \n Newegg - A great selection of NVMe SSDs on offer \n 1 TB \n SATA\n Teamgroup QX | 2.5-inch | 1 TB: $138.99 at Newegg It's a fair chunk of cash for a slow SSD but if you're looking for lots of storage and don't need a blazing fast NVMe drive, then this is the cheapest way of putting a 1 TB SSD in your gaming PC. Just don't expect it to sustain full speed under heavy workloads and you'll be fine.\n Key specs: 2.5-inch | 560 MB/s read | 500 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Save $59.49\n Kingston NV3 | 2280 NVMe | 1 TB: was $215.99 now $156.50 at Amazon With 6,000 MB/s read speed and 5,000 MB/s write speed, this is slower than the best but not as slow as the rest. It's still going to feel plenty fast for most use cases, particularly if you're replacing a Gen3 drive or using it as some extra backup space. Great for the cash, I reckon.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 6,000 MB/s read | 5,000 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Save $23.94\n Patriot P410 | 2280 NVMe | 1 TB: was $188.93 now $164.99 at Newegg This may not be the fastest drive, but in the midst of the memory supply crisis, it is at least relatively cheap compared to some. \n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 4,500 MB/s read | 5,000 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 5.0\n Save $14.75\n Crucial P510 | 2280 NVMe | 1 TB: was $183.33 now $168.58 at Newegg There's a weird wrinkle here in that you're buying this SSD from Amazon Germany rather than its American counterpart. If you're willing to roll the dice on delivery, this is a banging price for a PCIe 5.0 drive— check out our full review from last year .\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 5.0 | 11,000 MB/s read | 9,500 MB/s write \n Price check: Amazon $217.99 \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Save $71.89\n Crucial P310 | 2280 NVMe | 1 TB: was $239.99 now $168.10 at Amazon This DRAM-less drive is well-suited for stashing your operating system alongside a few games from your ever-growing Steam backlog. This is the version without a heatsink, which means you can slot it straight into a console, too.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 7,100 MB/s read | 6,000 MB/s write \n Price Check: Newegg $198.99 \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Save $10\n Biwin Black Opal NV7400 | 2280 NVMe | 1 TB: was $184.99 now $174.99 at Amazon Okay, so our tests demonstrate it's slightly slower than the advertised speeds , but the actual sequential performance still isn't bad. Couple that with a very reasonable price point, plus its practically frosty temps, and you might well be looking at your next upgrade.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 7,400 MB/s read | 6,500 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n Save $5.99\n Samsung 990 Evo Plus | 1 TB: was $239.99 now $234 at Newegg Samsung's 990 Evo Plus range has a lot going for it ( check out our review ). Along with Samsung's excellent Magician software, it's a really solid SSD to stick in any gaming PC.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 7,150 MB/s read | 6,300 MB/s write \n Price check: Amazon $237.00 \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 5.0\n Save 23% ($78.46)\n Samsung 9100 PRO | 2280 NVMe | 1 TB: was $339.99 now $261.53 at Newegg Yes, this is a pretty pricey 1 TB drive, but it's also a PCIe 5.0 SSD. Just get a load of those read/write speeds! If you're still not convinced, be sure to have a read of Zak's review .\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 5.0 | 14,700 MB/s read | 13,300 MB/s write \n Price check: Amazon $261.53 \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n Best SSD for gaming 2026 \n All our current recommendations \n 1. Best overall: \n WD_Black SN7100 \n 2. Best budget: \n Biwin Black Opal NV7400 \n 3. Best PCIe 5.0: \n WD_Black SN8100 \n 4. Best budget PCIe 5.0: \n Crucial P510 \n 5. Best 4 TB: \n TeamGroup MP44 \n 6. Best 8 TB: \n WD_Black SN850X \n 7. Best M.2 2230: \n Lexar Play 2230 \n 8. Best for PS5: \n Silicon Power XS70 \n 👉 Check out our full SSD for gaming guide 👈 \n 2 TB \n PCIe 4.0\n Crucial P310 | 2280 NVMe | 2 TB: $308.95 at Amazon This drive uses QLC memory and comes without DRAM cache, but it's still an impressively quick drive for the money, with strong sequential performance.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | Up to 7,100 MB/s read | up to 6,000 MB/s write \n Price check: Newegg $308.99 \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 5.0\n Save 40% ($270.97)\n Samsung 9100 PRO | 2280 NVMe | 2 TB: was $679.97 now $409 at Amazon Yes, this is a very pricey 2 TB drive, but it's also a PCIe 5.0 SSD. Just get a load of those read/write speeds! If you're still not convinced, be sure to have a read of Zak's review .\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 5.0 | 14,700 MB/s read | 13,300 MB/s write \n Price check: Newegg $404.95 \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n 4 TB or more \n PCIe 4.0\n Save 1% ($3)\n Silicon Power UD90 | 2280 NVMe | 4 TB: was $284.79 now $281.79 at Newegg With a sequential read/write of 5,000 MB/s and 4,500 MB/s, this isn't what you'd call slow and should be fine for gaming, plus it comes from a reliable brand.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 5,000 MB/s read | 4,500 MB/s write \n Price check: Amazon $549.97 \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Save $184\n Teamgroup MP44 | 2280 NVMe | 4 TB: was $509.99 now $325.99 at Newegg This Team Group boasts some mighty specs for the money, though it's actually the same hardware as in the Lexar NM790 . The sequential read/write speeds are extremely high, but it can only sustain them over short bursts. Read our full review of this SSD for more info.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 7,400 MB/s read | 6,900 MB/s write \n Price check: Amazon $512.99 \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Save $26\n WD Blue SN5000 | 2280 NVMe | 4 TB: was $518.49 now $492.49 at Newegg What it lacks in outright NVMe speed, this SN5000 makes up for with capacity. It's a lot of cash for four terabytes, especially with slower speeds, but if you consider the cost of two terabytes, it's not actually the worst deal under the current circumstances.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 5,500 MB/s read | 4,000 MB/s write speed \n Price check: Amazon $514.99 \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Teamgroup MP44Q | 2280 NVMe | 4 TB: $496.99 at Amazon This is a good drive for bulking out your storage, especially for a Steam library. It uses QLC NAND rather than TLC, which the slightly more expensive MP44 also uses, and that means it is a bit slower. Thankfully, that won't really be a problem unless you're moving huge files around on the regular.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | Up to 7,400 MB/s read | Up to 6,200 MB/s write \n Price check: Newegg (Out of Stock) \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n External SSDs \n Save $13\n Adata SE880 | USB 3.2 Gen2x2 | 1 TB: was $162.99 now $149.99 at Amazon It's not often you see a 2000 MB/s external SSD going for this sorta money. That's speedier than most in this price bracket. We've tested it , and while we don't love the peak transfer speed dropping so quickly, the size and (relative) price help alleviate some of our concerns.\n Key specs: USB 3.2 Gen2x2 | 2,000 MB/s read and write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n SSK Portable SSD | USB 3.2 Gen2 | 1 TB: $169.99 at Amazon You can save some money here with this slightly slower external SSD. It's still plenty quick for most use cases, including regular small transfers. It also has a metal shell and 1-meter shockproof design.\n Key specs: USB 3.2 Gen 2 | 1,050 MB/s read | 1,000 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n Save $46.04\n Samsung T9 Portable SSD | 1 TB: was $287.99 now $241.95 at Amazon Not only is this a nippy external SSD, but it also sticks darn close to those advertised peak speeds seemingly endlessly. It does this without getting all that toasty either, thanks to Samsung's \"Dynamic Thermal Guard.\"\n Key specs: USB 3.2 Gen 2x2 | 2,000 MB/s read and write \n Price check: Newegg $257.99 \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n SSD deals in the UK \n Where are the best SSD deals?\n 1 TB PCIe 4.0 SSD: Crucial P310 | £160 @ Amazon \n 2 TB PCIe 4.0 SSD: Crucial P310 | £200 @ Amazon \n Amazon UK - Save on HDDs, SDDs, and external drives \n Scan - SATA drives on sale \n Overclockers - Lots of SSDs to choose from \n Currys - Plenty of options from SATA to NVMe \n Laptops Direct - Surprisingly not just laptops, SSDs too! \n CCL - SSDs for bargain prices \n SATA\n Save £2.01\n Lexar NS100 | 2.5-inch | 512 GB: was £81.99 now £79.98 at Amazon For just storing documents, this small SATA SSD is a lot faster than a traditional HDD, and Lexar makes reliable and robust SSDs. You're still better off getting an NVMe M.2 SSD for games and your operating system, though.\n Key specs: 2.5-inch | SATA 6 Gb/s | 550 MB/s read | 520 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Biwin Black Opal NV7400 | 2280 NVMe | 1 TB: £145.99 at Amazon Okay, so our tests demonstrate it's slightly slower than the advertised speeds , but the actual sequential performance still isn't bad. Couple that with a very reasonable price point, plus its practically frosty temps, and you might well be looking at your next upgrade.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 7,400 MB/s read | 6,500 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Save £2.20\n Crucial P310 | 2280 NVMe | 1 TB: was £162 now £159.80 at Amazon While this is QLC memory and comes without a DRAM cache, it's still an impressively quick drive for the money, with strong sequential performance.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 7,100 MB/s read | 6,000 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Lexar NQ790 | 2280 NVMe | 1 TB: £159.99 at Amazon This drive's endurance rating of 600 TBW is pretty decent for 1 TB. The price, the five year warranty, and the read/write speeds also make this an appealing option for bulking out your storage.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 7,000 MB/s read | 5,000 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Acer Predator GM7 | 2280 NVMe | 1 TB: £165.99 at Amazon Though not as fast nor always as cheap as the Biwin, this drive does happen to be supported by the Biwin Intelligence performance management software. That's some consolation, right?\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 7,200 MB/s read | 6,200 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Save £10\n WD_Black SN7100 2280 | 2280 NVMe | 1 TB: was £179.99 now £169.99 at Overclockers This SSD is no slouch and still offers some of the best-in-class 4K read performance ( read our full review here ). Though this drops the DDR4 DRAM cache and instead opts for a single-sided design, it's still got the same versatile form factor that perfectly suits this heat-sinkless drive to PCs, consoles, and laptops.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 7,250 MB/s read | 6,900 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n WD_Black SN850X | 2280 NVMe | 1 TB: £221.52 at Amazon This is one of our favourite SSDs for gaming right now. Unlike the cheaper SN770, the SN850X encapsulates the best PCIe 4.0 offers in terms of performance ( check out our review ). That makes it a great fit for a boot drive with space to spare for your game library, and at this price, we're happy to pay the premium for its higher speed.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 7,300 MB/s read | 6,300 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Biwin Black Opal NV7400 | 2280 NVMe | 2 TB: £268.99 at Amazon Okay, so our tests demonstrate it's slightly slower than the advertised speeds , but the actual sequential performance still isn't bad. Couple that with a very reasonable price point, plus its practically frosty temps, and you might well be looking at your next upgrade.\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 7,400 MB/s read | 6,500 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n PCIe 4.0\n Acer Predator GM7 | 2280 NVMe | 2 TB: £275.99 at Amazon Though not as fast nor always as cheap as the Biwin, this drive does happen to be supported by the Biwin Intelligence performance management software. That's some consolation, right?\n Key specs: NVMe | PCIe 4.0 | 7,200 MB/s read | 6,200 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n Back to top ^ \n UK external SSD deals \n Save £26\n Acer Predator GP30 | USB 3.2 Gen2x2 | 1 TB: was £215.99 now £189.99 at Amazon Though chunky, you can definitely forgive this drive's more rugged design in light of its 5-year limited warranty. It's not as fast as other options, but still worth considering at this price point.\n Key specs: USB 3.2 Gen2x2 | 2,000 MB/s read | 2,000 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n Crucial X10 Pro | USB 3.2 Gen2x2 | 2 TB: £289.99 at Amazon If you need a small external SSD but don't want to sacrifice capacity or performance, then Crucial's X10 Pro is a great choice. It's the same size as the X6 model but has double the read/write performance, though you will need to plug it into a 20 Gbps USB port to reach full speed.\n Key specs: USB 3.2 Gen2x2 | 2,100 MB/s read | 2,000 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n Acer Predator GP30 | USB 3.2 Gen2x2 | 2 TB: £295.99 at Amazon Though chunky, you can definitely forgive this drive's more rugged design in light of its 5-year limited warranty.\n Key specs: USB 3.2 Gen2x2 | 2,000 MB/s read | 2,000 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n Sandisk Extreme | USB 3.2 Gen2 | 2 TB: £329.99 at Amazon A slower drive but more affordable than most with 2 TB of capacity. If you're not worried about fast transfers, this is well suited to most tasks without a time limit.\n Key specs: USB 3.2 Gen2 | 1,050 MB/s read | 1,000 MB/s write \n Read more Read less ▼ \n View Deal \n Back to top ^ \n Get the best curated deals in your inbox every week\n The PC Gamer Circuit Breaker newsletter breaks down the latest PC gaming tech, and finds you the best deals on the best products\nJess Kinghorn\n Hardware Writer\n Jess has been writing about games for over ten years, spending a significant chunk of that time working on print publications PLAY and Official PlayStation Magazine. When she’s not investigating all things hardware here, she's either cons