   "text": "10GbE SSD NAS Under $500 in 2026: Which Models Actually Qualify?-LincPlus \n Products \n Most popular \n LincStation \n Comparison \n LincStation E1\n LincStation S1\n LincStation N2\n LincStation N1\n LincStation E1\n LincStation S1\n LincStation N2\n LincStation N1\n LincOS \n Download \n Support \n Discover \n Blog \n About LincPlus \n Products \n Most popular \n LincStation \n contrast \n LincOS \n Download \n Support \n Discover \n Blog \n About LincPlus \n Store \n Official Store \n Official German Store \n Middle East Distributor \n Store \n Official Store \n Official German Store \n Middle East Distributor \n Login \n | -->\n register \n Nickname \n | -->\n quit \n EN \n DE \n JA \n Home \n 10GbE SSD NAS Under $500 in 2026: Which Models Actually Qualify?\n Release date:\n Jul 22,2026\n In this article\n Share this article \n A sub-$500 NAS with true 10GbE and an SSD-based storage pool sounds easy to find. In practice, the list is surprisingly short. \n Many products shown in searches for “budget 10GbE NAS” use 5GbE, combine two 2.5GbE ports, rely primarily on hard drives, or only fall below $500 during a limited promotion. Those systems may still be good choices, but they do not all meet the same requirement. \n For this guide, a NAS must pass three checks: \n It has a true 10GbE network connection. \n It can use SSDs as the main storage pool, not only as a cache. \n Its normal diskless price is below $500 in the United States. \n Based on the widely available products we could verify, the LincStation N2 is the clearest match. A few other models come close, but miss the price, network, or storage requirement. \n What counts as a 10GbE SSD NAS? \n 1. A true 10GbE connection \n A qualifying system needs a single 10GbE link, either through 10GBASE-T RJ45 or SFP+. \n A 5GbE port does not qualify. Two 2.5GbE ports also do not provide a single 5Gbps or 10Gbps transfer by default. Link aggregation can distribute separate network sessions across multiple ports, but one file transfer is normally limited by the speed of one connection. \n That distinction matters for people editing large files directly from a NAS or moving large project folders during the working day. \n 2. SSDs as primary storage \n An SSD NAS should be able to store the main data pool on solid-state drives. \n A conventional four-bay hard-drive NAS with two M.2 cache slots is not the same thing. Cache can improve frequently accessed data, application loading, and metadata work, but the main files still live on the hard-drive array. \n This guide includes systems with M.2 NVMe or 2.5-inch SSD storage that can be used as the primary pool. \n 3. A diskless price below $500 \n The comparison uses the chassis price before SSDs, taxes, and shipping. \n We also distinguish between a normal listed price and a short promotional price. A product that occasionally drops below $500 may be a good deal, but it should not be treated as a consistently available sub-$500 option. \n Candidate comparison \n Model Price checked Network Main storage options Result \n LincStation N2 $405 direct 1× 10GbE RJ45 4× M.2 NVMe + 2× 2.5-inch SATA Meets all three criteria \n TerraMaster F8 SSD $659.99 direct 1× 10GBASE-T 8× M.2 NVMe Above the $500 limit \n TerraMaster F4 SSD Check current retailer price 1× 5GbE RJ45 4× M.2 NVMe Does not meet the 10GbE requirement \n UGREEN NASync DXP4800 Varies by retailer 2× 2.5GbE 4× SATA + 2× M.2 No single 10GbE port; HDD-primary design \n UGREEN NASync DXP4800 Plus $619.99 direct 1× 10GbE + 1× 2.5GbE 4× SATA + 2× M.2 Above $500 and primarily designed around SATA bays \n UniFi UNAS Pro $499 direct 1× 10G SFP+ 7× 2.5/3.5-inch SATA Meets the price and network checks, but is not an all-flash design \n TerraMaster F8 SSD Plus $719.99 direct 1× 10GBASE-T 8× M.2 NVMe Above the $500 limit \n AOOSTAR WTR Max i5-1235U $559 barebone Dual 10G SFP+ + dual 2.5GbE 6× SATA + 5× M.2 Above $500 before memory and software \n The table is not a ranking of overall product quality. It only shows whether each model meets the narrow definition used in this article. \n A hard-drive NAS may be the better choice for a 40TB archive. A more expensive eight-slot SSD NAS may be the better choice for a growing production team. The right purchase depends on the workload, not simply on which row passes the most filters. \n The model that clearly fits the requirement \n LincStation N2 \n The LincStation N2 combines the following hardware in a compact diskless system: \n Intel N100 processor with four cores and four threads \n 16GB LPDDR5 memory \n 128GB eMMC system storage \n One 10GbE RJ45 port \n Four M.2 2280 NVMe slots \n Two 2.5-inch SATA SSD bays \n Unraid preinstalled with a Starter license \n At the time of checking, the LincPlus store listed the diskless N2 at $405 . \n The combination is aimed at home labs, creators, media servers, Docker applications, backups, and users who want a compact SSD NAS without adding a separate 10GbE card. \n Where the N2 fits well \n The N2 is a practical option for: \n Large-file transfers from one or a few workstations \n Video and photo project storage \n Docker containers and self-hosted services \n Jellyfin, Plex, Immich, and home-server applications \n Quiet desktop or shelf installations \n Users who already plan to use Unraid \n Its six storage positions also allow more flexibility than a four-slot M.2-only design. A user could build an NVMe pool, use the two SATA bays for additional SSD capacity, or separate application data from bulk storage. \n Where the N2 has limits \n The Intel N100 is an efficient entry-level processor, not a workstation-class CPU. \n It is well suited to file services and lightweight containers, but users planning several continuously active virtual machines, heavy software compilation, or demanding multi-user compute workloads should consider a higher-core platform. \n Compact Intel N100 systems also have a limited PCIe lane budget. Installing four NVMe drives does not mean every drive can operate at its maximum advertised speed at the same time. For a typical single-client 10GbE workload, that may not be a practical problem. For several users hitting different drives simultaneously, platform bandwidth matters more. \n The closest alternatives \n TerraMaster F8 SSD \n The F8 SSD provides eight M.2 NVMe slots and a 10GBASE-T connection. Its main advantage is capacity expansion: users can add more M.2 drives without moving to an external enclosure. \n The official US store listed it at $659.99 when checked, placing it outside this article’s budget. Retailer promotions may occasionally narrow the gap, so it is worth comparing current prices before ordering. \n The eight M.2 slots use PCIe 3.0 ×1 connections per drive. That is enough to build an aggregate pool capable of using a 10GbE connection, but it is not the same as giving every SSD a full-width NVMe link. \n TerraMaster F4 SSD \n The F4 SSD is a compact four-slot all-flash NAS, but its published network interface is 5GbE , not 10GbE. \n That does not make it a poor product. For many home users, 5GbE provides more than enough bandwidth with lower networking costs. It simply does not meet the true 10GbE definition used here. \n UGREEN NASync DXP4800 Plus \n The DXP4800 Plus includes a real 10GbE port and a capable Intel Pentium Gold 8505 processor. Its four main bays are designed for SATA hard drives or SSDs, with two additional M.2 slots. \n At $619.99 on the official US store when checked, it exceeds the $500 limit. It is also better described as a flexible hybrid NAS than a purpose-built compact all-flash system. \n UniFi UNAS Pro \n The UNAS Pro costs $499 and includes a 10G SFP+ connection, so it meets two of the three checks. \n Its seven bays are designed for 2.5-inch or 3.5-inch SATA drives. It is a strong fit for rack-mounted shared storage, particularly in an existing UniFi environment, but it is not an M.2 all-flash NAS. \n AOOSTAR WTR Max \n The Intel WTR Max starts at $559 and offers dual 10G SFP+, dual 2.5GbE, six SATA positions, and five M.2 slots. \n It offers considerably more expansion than the systems below $500, but the entry price is for a barebone configuration. Memory, storage, and the operating system must be added separately. \n The chassis is only part of the budget \n A $405 or $499 NAS is not a complete all-flash system. The SSDs, networking equipment, and client hardware can easily account for most of the final cost. \n Before choosing the enclosure, calculate the full build. \n 1. SSDs \n Every system in this comparison is diskless. \n The final SSD cost depends on: \n Capacity per drive \n TLC or QLC NAND \n Endurance rating \n Whether the drive includes DRAM \n Thermal design \n Warranty \n Current promotions \n For many buyers, starting with two larger SSDs is more practical than filling every slot with smaller drives. It reduces the initial cost and leaves space for later expansion. \n The correct layout depends on whether the priority is capacity, redundancy, or performance. RAID and mirrored pools protect against a drive failure, but they are not a substitute for a separate backup. \n 2. The 10GbE connection \n There are two common ways to connect a 10GbE NAS. \n Through a switch \n A 10GbE switch is useful when several computers need high-speed access. \n A typical setup may require: \n A 10GbE switch \n A compatible network card for each workstation \n DAC cables, Cat6a cables, or transceivers \n Correct SFP+ or 10GBASE-T compatibility \n This is the more flexible option, but it adds cost, power consumption, heat, and configuration work. \n Directly to one workstation \n When only one computer needs the full 10GbE connection, direct attachment is often the simplest option. \n Connect the NAS directly to the workstation with a compatible cable, then assign both 10GbE interfaces static addresses on a separate subnet. The normal network connection can continue to handle internet access and other devices. \n For example: \n Workstation 10GbE interface: 10.0.10.1 \n NAS 10GbE interface: 10.0.10.2 \n Subnet mask: 255.255.255.0 \n No gateway or DNS on the direct 10GbE interfaces \n This avoids buying a switch solely for one fast client. \n 3. Client-side networking \n The computer also needs a 10GbE interface. \n Some workstations and higher-end motherboards include one. Many do not. Before ordering the NAS, check whether the client needs: \n A PCIe 10GbE network card \n A Thunderbolt-to-10GbE adapter \n An SFP+ card \n A 10GBASE-T card \n Updated drivers \n The NAS cannot deliver 10GbE performance through a 1GbE or 2.5GbE client port. \n 4. The operating system \n Operating-system cost depends on the platform. \n TrueNAS and OpenMediaVault are available without a license fee. Unraid uses paid licenses. The current Unraid lineup includes Starter, Unleashed, and Lifetime options, with different storage-device and update terms. \n The LincStation N2 includes an Unraid Starter license, which is useful for buyers who already prefer Unraid. Buyers who intend to install another operating system should evaluate the hardware independently of the bundled license. \n Three realistic build approaches \n Exact SSD prices change frequently, so the examples below focus on configuration rather than claiming a fixed all-in total. \n Entry build \n LincStation N2 \n Two NVMe SSDs \n Direct 10GbE connection to one workstation \n Existing home network used for internet and other devices \n This is the lowest-cost practical route for one user who wants fast local project storage and room to expand. \n Balanced build \n LincStation N2 \n Four NVMe SSDs \n A redundant or parity-protected storage layout \n A 10GbE network card for the workstation \n Direct connection or a small 10GbE switch \n This configuration suits creators, home labs, and users running several services from the same NAS. \n Expansion-focused build \n An eight-slot system such as the TerraMaster F8 SSD or F8 SSD Plus \n Four SSDs initially, with empty slots reserved for growth \n A multi-client 10GbE switch \n A higher-core CPU if virtual machines are important \n This costs more upfront, but reduces the chance of replacing the enclosure when the storage pool grows. \n When 10GbE makes a real difference \n 10GbE is most useful when the workload can sustain large sequential transfers. \n Good examples include: \n Editing high-bitrate video directly from the NAS \n Moving large video masters or disk images \n Working with large photography catalogs \n Hosting virtual-machine disks over the network \n Copying 100GB or larger datasets during the working day \n Supporting several active clients on an SSD pool \n A well-configured 10GbE connection can be several times faster than gigabit Ethernet for these tasks. Actual performance still depends on the storage layout, protocol, client hardware, filesystem, cooling, and network configuration. \n When 2.5GbE is enough \n Many home NAS workloads do not need 10GbE. \n A 2.5GbE connection is usually sufficient for: \n Jellyfin or Plex streaming \n Phone and computer backups \n General document sharing \n Photo backup \n A small hard-drive array \n Overnight backup jobs \n One or two home users \n A typical 4K Blu-ray remux uses only a small fraction of a gigabit link. Faster storage and networking do not improve video quality, and they do not solve client compatibility, Wi-Fi congestion, or transcoding limitations. \n For these workloads, 2.5GbE usually offers a better balance of cost, heat, and simplicity. \n Why a 10GbE NAS may not reach 10GbE speed \n A 10GbE port does not guarantee 1GB/s file transfers. \n Common limits include: \n Storage-pool performance \n One slow SATA SSD, an inefficient parity layout, or a pool performing background work may limit transfers. \n Small files \n Thousands of small files create metadata and open-and-close overhead. They rarely transfer at the same rate as one large video file. \n SSD thermal throttling \n NVMe drives in a compact enclosure can become hot during sustained writes. Once a controller reaches its thermal limit, performance may drop until the drive cools. \n Use suitable thermal pads or heatsinks where supported, maintain airflow, and check drive temperatures during long transfers. \n Network configuration \n Possible causes include: \n Incorrect drivers \n Receive Side Scaling disabled \n A damaged or unsuitable cable \n Mismatched MTU settings \n A slow transceiver \n An older PCIe slot limiting the client network card \n Start troubleshooting with one large file. That separates network and storage throughput from small-file overhead. \n Which type of buyer should choose each system? \n Creator working directly from the NAS \n Prioritize: \n True 10GbE \n SSD primary storage \n Reliable cooling \n A direct connection or low-latency switch \n Enough capacity for active projects \n The LincStation N2 is a cost-effective option for one or a few users. An eight-slot system may be a better long-term choice when project capacity is expected to grow quickly. \n Home lab and container user \n Prioritize: \n Memory \n Docker support \n Low idle power \n Quiet operation \n Flexible storage pools \n Easy backup of application data \n The N2’s Intel N100 and included Unraid license are well matched to file services and lightweight containers. Heavy virtual-machine users should choose a higher-core platform. \n Small business with several active users \n Prioritize: \n CPU headroom \n Memory expansion \n Multi-client network design \n Backup and recovery \n Warranty and support \n Storage expansion \n A sub-$500 N100 or N95 system can work for file sharing, but a growing business may benefit from a more powerful processor and additional drive positions. \n Large media archive \n Prioritize cost per terabyte rather than flash performance. \n For tens of terabytes of movies and backups, a hard-drive NAS with NVMe used for applications, metadata, or cache is usually more economical than an all-flash build. \n Buying checklist \n Before placing an order, confirm the following: \n Is the network port really 10GbE? \n Check the manufacturer’s specification page rather than relying on a search-result title. \n Can SSDs be used as the primary storage pool? \n Do not assume M.2 slots are intended for primary data; some systems limit them to cache. \n Does the listed price include drives? \n Most NAS products are sold diskless. \n How are the M.2 slots connected? \n The number of slots does not reveal the PCIe bandwidth available to each drive. \n Does the workstation have 10GbE? \n Add the cost of a network card or adapter when necessary. \n Do you need a switch? \n One fast workstation can often use a direct connection. \n What operating system will you use? \n Include license costs and check hardware compatibility. \n How will you back up the NAS? \n RAID, parity, and mirrors do not protect against accidental deletion, malware, theft, or enclosure failure. \n Can the enclosure cool the selected SSDs? \n Check heatsink clearance, thermal-pad support, and sustained-load temperatures. \n Was the price checked recently? \n NAS and SSD promotions change often. \n FAQ \n Is there a true 10GbE all-flash NAS under $500? \n Yes, but the selection is limited. Among the widely available US models verified for this guide, the LincStation N2 met all three criteria at its listed price. \n Availability and pricing change, so check current product pages before ordering. \n Do I need a 10GbE switch? \n Not necessarily. \n A switch is useful when several computers need high-speed access. For one workstation, a direct connection between the NAS and computer is often simpler and less expensive. \n Is 10GbE worth it for a home NAS? \n It depends on the workload. \n 10GbE is useful for large files, direct video editing, virtual-machine storage, and active SSD-based workflows. It is usually unnecessary for media streaming, ordinary backups, and general home file sharing. \n Why is my 10GbE NAS only transferring at 300MB/s? \n Possible causes include the storage pool, small files, SSD thermal throttling, network-card drivers, cable quality, MTU mismatch, or a client-side PCIe limitation. \n Test with one large file first, then monitor disk temperature, CPU use, and network utilization. \n Do the NAS units include SSDs? \n No. The systems discussed here are sold diskless unless a specific bundle says otherwise. \n Is an all-flash NAS better than an HDD NAS? \n Not for every workload. \n All-flash systems are compact, quiet, responsive, and strong at random I/O. Hard drives remain more economical for large media archives and backup capacity. A hybrid design is often the most practical option. \n Final recommendation \n The LincStation N2 is a strong fit for buyers looking for a compact SSD NAS with true 10GbE, six storage positions, and Unraid included at a sub-$500 chassis price. \n It is not the right choice for every buyer. Users who need eight M.2 slots should compare the TerraMaster F8 SSD range. Users building a large rack-mounted archive may be better served by the UniFi UNAS Pro or another hard-drive platform. Users running several demanding virtual machines should consider a system with more CPU cores and expandable memory. \n The useful question is not simply, “Which NAS has 10GbE?” \n It is: \n Will your storage pool, workstation, workload, and budget make meaningful use of it? \n View the LincStation N2 specifications \n Share this article \n Previous\n Best NAS for Unraid in 2026: Build One, Reuse One, or Buy One Ready-Made?\n Next\n Prebuilt Unraid NAS vs DIY in 2026: Is Building Your Own Still Worth It?\n More Blogs\n View all >\n %E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%EF%BC%8C%E6%9C%80%EF%BC%8C%E4%BC%98%E7%A7%80%EF%BC%8C%E5%A2%9E%E5%BC%BA%EF%BC%8C%E4%B8%80%E6%B5%81%EF%BC%8C%E5%8D%93%E8%B6%8A%EF%BC%8C%E9%A2%86%E5%85%88%EF%BC%8C%E5%85%88%E8%BF%9B%EF%BC%8C%E5%BC%95%E9%A2%86\n Sorry，当前栏目暂无内容!\n 您可以查看其他栏目或返回 首页 \n Sorry，The current column has no content!\n You can view other columns or return Home \n Consumer \n LincStation S1 \n LincStation N2 \n LincStation N1 \n Where to buy \n Official Store \n Amazon \n Official German Store \n Support \n Product Support \n After-Sales Policy \n Download \n Partners \n Contact Us \n Become a Reseller \n About \n Blog \n About LincPlus \n Legal Documents \n Privacy Policy \n EULA \n LincOS Privacy Policy \n Permissions Guide \n Subscribe\n Get the latest news from Lincplus\n Privacy Policy ｜ Cookie Settings ｜ Cookie Policy ｜ Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information",
